An absolutely positioned box that would be inline-level in flow takes
its static position from where a line would put it. Ladybird keeps such
a box in the inline content of its parent: Where block-level siblings
follow, it goes into an anonymous block with the floats and the inline
content before it, and the line there places it past the floats,
where the text alignment says.

Blink gets it without an anonymous block. Its BlockLayoutAlgorithm::
HandleOutOfFlowPositioned() takes the inline offset of the box
from CalculateOutOfFlowStaticInlineLevelOffset(), which finds the
layout opportunity beside the floats and applies the text alignment.
WebKit’s RenderBlockFlow::adjustPositionedBlock() takes that offset
from staticInlinePositionForOriginalDisplayInline(). Gecko gives
every out-of-flow frame a placeholder in a line, where ReflowInput::
CalculateHypotheticalPosition() reads the static position.

In Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0 and WebKit 26.5, the test’s 27
cases come out the same. The three disagree where a float follows the
box, and on “text-indent”, so the test leaves those out.
